p9share -- share directories between machines over 9P, through ssh

On the machine that has the files, as yourself:

    p9share export /srv/projects bob.example.org

On the machine that should show them, once:

    sudo p9share mount alice projects /mnt/projects

That is all.  The exporting machine dials out to the other with ssh, so the
receiving side needs no open port and no server for you to configure.  A daemon
that starts at boot keeps the tunnel up (restarting it when it drops) and keeps the
mount right: mounted while the tunnel is there, unmounted when it is not.  The user
on the receiving machine switches it without sudo:

    p9share ro projects        read-only
    p9share rw projects        read-write
    p9share off projects       not mounted

    p9share status             what is shared, what is mounted, what is running

Several directories, several machines, and both directions at once all work; each
is one more line.  Nothing happens on a machine until something is configured on it.

How it works: the exporter runs diod (the 9P2000.L server) as you, unprivileged, on a
private unix socket; a small relay in front of it supplies the numeric uid an
unprivileged diod needs; ssh -R hands that socket to the other machine, into the
user's ~/.p9share/.  The receiver's root daemon mounts it with the kernel's 9p client.

Requirements: Debian or Ubuntu (WSL2 too), openssh-client; key-based
ssh from the exporter to the receiver (BatchMode: no password prompts).

Security: the export is the directory you name, with your rights, and the mount is
nosuid,nodev.  The 9P socket on the receiving machine belongs to that machine's user;
any local user there who can reach the mount point sees the files as you.  Do not
export a directory to a machine whose users you do not trust.

On WSL2 there is no systemd: sudo p9share wsl-boot puts the daemon in /etc/wsl.conf.

See INSTALL, p9share(8), p9share-exports(5), p9share-mounts(5).  License: ISC.
